For constructing a biventricular myocardial volume mesh from the model’s surface mesh results, I would use these files:

  For ED

  data/<subject>/vtks/F_LVendo_ED.vtk
  data/<subject>/vtks/F_LVepi_ED.vtk
  data/<subject>/vtks/S_RV_ED.vtk
  data/<subject>/vtks/N_RVepi_ED.vtk

  For ES

  data/<subject>/vtks/F_LVendo_ES.vtk
  data/<subject>/vtks/F_LVepi_ES.vtk
  data/<subject>/vtks/S_RV_ES.vtk
  data/<subject>/vtks/N_RVepi_ES.vtk

  Do not mix ED and ES surfaces.

  Why These Files

  LV myocardium volume =
  outer boundary: F_LVepi_ED.vtk
  inner cavity:   F_LVendo_ED.vtk

  RV myocardium volume =
  outer boundary: N_RVepi_ED.vtk
  inner cavity:   S_RV_ED.vtk

  Rationale:

  - F_LVendo_* and F_LVepi_* are fitted LV template-correspondence surfaces.
    They are smoother and more consistent than raw marching-cubes surfaces.

  - S_RV_* is the final fitted RV surface used for signed-distance analysis.
    In this pipeline, it is the best final RV endocardial/cavity surface.

  - N_RVepi_* is the RV outer/wall surface. The code does not produce
    F_RVepi_* or S_RVepi_*, so N_RVepi_* is the correct available RV epicardial/wall boundary.

  - These four surfaces describe the two myocardial shells needed for a volume mesh: LV wall and RV wall.

  Pipeline Logic

  PHsegmentation_ED.gipl
          |
          v
  mesh extraction
          |
          v
  raw surfaces
  LVendo_ED.vtk
  LVepi_ED.vtk
  RV_ED.vtk
  RVepi_ED.vtk
          |
          v
  template fitting / registration
          |
          v
  recommended volume-mesh boundaries
  F_LVendo_ED.vtk
  F_LVepi_ED.vtk
  S_RV_ED.vtk
  N_RVepi_ED.vtk

  Files I Would Not Use As Primary Boundaries

  LVmyo_ED.vtk
  F_LVmyo_ED.vtk
  S_LVmyo_ED.vtk
  W_LVmyo_ED.vtk
  C_LVmyo_ED.vtk

  These are useful for LV myocardium scalar analysis, but for volume meshing it is clearer to use the two explicit LV boundaries:

  F_LVendo_ED.vtk
  F_LVepi_ED.vtk

  Also avoid using only:

  RV_ED.vtk
  RVepi_ED.vtk

  unless you specifically want raw segmentation-derived surfaces. They are more anatomically direct,
  but less smoothed and less template-consistent.

  Practical Meshing Structure

  F_LVepi_ED.vtk
          \
           -> LV myocardium tetrahedral region         inner hole / LV blood pool boundary
          /
  F_LVendo_ED.vtk


  N_RVepi_ED.vtk
          \
           -> RV myocardium tetrahedral region         inner hole / RV blood pool boundary
          /
  S_RV_ED.vtk

  Before tetrahedralization, you should still check:

  closed surfaces
  normal orientation
  self-intersections
  LV/RV overlap near septum
  small holes
  duplicate points/faces

  For TetGen/Gmsh-style meshing, the inner cavity surfaces usually need opposite orientation from the outer surfaces.